  "question": {
    "text": "The electric field E is measured at a point <math><mi>P</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>0,0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>)</mo></math> , generated due to various charge distributions and the dependence of E on d is found to be different for different charge distributions. List-I contains different relations between E and d. List-II describes different electric charge distributions, along with their locations. Match the functions in List-I with the related charge distributions in List-II.<table border=\"1\">\t<tbody>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\t\t\t<td>LIST -I</td>\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\t\t\t<td>LIST -II</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td>A)</td>\t\t\t<td>E is independent of d</td>\t\t\t<td>P)</td>\t\t\t<td>A point charge at the origin</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td>B)</td>\t\t\t<td><math><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></td>\t\t\t<td>Q)</td>\t\t\t<td>A small dipole with point charges <math><mi>Q</mi></math> at <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>0,0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfenced><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi><mo>&#8810;</mo><mi>d</mi></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td>C)</td>\t\t\t<td><math><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></math></td>\t\t\t<td>R)</td>\t\t\t<td>An infinite line charge coincident with the x &ndash; axis, with uniform linear charge density <math><mi>&#955;</mi></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td>D)</td>\t\t\t<td><math><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></math></td>\t\t\t<td>S)</td>\t\t\t<td>Two infinite wires carrying uniform linear charge density parallel to the x &ndash; axis. The one along <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> has a charge density <math><mo>+</mo><mi>&#955;</mi></math> and the one along <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> has a charge density <math><mo>&#8211;</mo><mi>&#955;</mi></math> . Take <math><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi><mo>&#8810;</mo><mi>d</mi></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\t\t\t<td>T)</td>\t\t\t<td>Infinite plane charge coincident with the xy &ndash; plane with uniform surface charge density</td>\t\t</tr>\t</tbody></table>",
